WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.479
When people socialize with the wrong crowd,
bad things can happen.

00:00:05.479 --> 00:00:12.380
They may form questionable values and develop
poor attitudes towards law and order.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.109
And if their friends are criminals, they naturally
learn from them and over time adopt their

00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:18.550
way of life.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.689
Psychologically, this process is known as
differential association.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.590
Differential association theory is the work
of Edwin Sutherland, who spent his life studying

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:38.760
principles of criminology, and found 9 distinct
precepts.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.830
To learn how this works in real life, let's
see how Robbin, a shy 14 year old boy, ends

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.460
up in jail.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.970
Criminal behavior is learned.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.070
Robbin learns it after meeting a new friend.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:01:01.240
It is learned through interaction and conversation
with others, often supported by storytelling.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.360
This learning process takes place primarily
in small and intimate groups.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.220
Books, video games, and mass media have little
impact.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.570
The learning of criminal behavior includes
sharing insights and techniques as well as

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:20.780
reasons and attitudes that favor criminal
actions.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.350
Motives are learned by dividing the legal
system into good laws and bad laws.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:29.600
Robbin learns that the laws protecting poor
people’s property are good, while the laws

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.390
protecting rich people’s property are bad.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.840
We become delinquent once our rationale for
breaking the law outweighs our rationale for

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:39.990
respecting it.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:47.240
Robbin steals once he believes that he needs
to take from the rich to give to the poor.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:52.690
Our learning experiences vary in frequency,
duration, the priority we give them, and how

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:53.950
intense they are.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:01.590
Robbin’s time as a criminal is over when
he and his friends all get arrested.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:04.760
Learning criminal behavior is like learning
anything else.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:12.950
Robbin learns this just like his sister learns
math — the same mechanisms are involved.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:18.150
Although criminal behavior is an expression
of one’s needs and values, it is not explained

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.430
by them.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:26.470
Non-criminal behavior can also stem from the
exact same needs and values.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:32.550
Edwin Sutherland was an American sociologist
whose work had a big impact on criminal policy.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:37.010
His theory recognizes that criminal attitudes
can be learnt socially.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.980
Consequently, they can also be unlearned.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:48.350
In other words, Sutherland helped us understand
that kids like Robbin can be rehabilitated.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.569
What do you think?

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:54.550
How can we help keep society free from crimes
that happen, on the street and in the top

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.900
floors of big corporations?

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.000
And if Sutherland was right, is jail the right
place for young offenders?

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:18.670
